{"parentUuid":"02ed42af-8e3f-4fd2-9a97-b02cee665324","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_012k7jczdmN2xuibxpnCbCiG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"SQLite has two files side-by-side when in WAL mode:\n\n- **`store.db`** — main DB file. Stable page data.\n- **`store.db-wal`** — \"Write-Ahead Log\". Every new write appends here first, not to main DB. Grows with each transaction. Was 300kB in our test.\n- **`store.db-shm`** — shared-memory index for WAL readers. Ephemeral.\n\nOn next reader open, SQLite merges WAL frames into main DB. Then truncates WAL. This merge = **WAL checkpoint**.\n\n`PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE)` forces it now: reads every WAL frame, writes to main DB pages, resets WAL to size 0.\n\nEffect on our archive:\n```\nbefore: db=4kB    wal=300kB   shm=32kB    → ship ~340kB\nafter:  db=~60kB  wal=0       shm=0       → ship ~60kB\n```\n\n~5-6× smaller. No data loss — WAL contents now inside store.db.\n\nRuns on a **copy** of the files, not cursor's live DB (cursor may have the WAL open). Copy → checkpoint → read → archive.\n\nCommit `c6e9c42d8` noted \"Cursor's next open checkpoints the WAL\" — meaning cursor does this on restore-side. But ship-side we still pay WAL bytes in git. Checkpointing ship-side means git stores just the merged DB.\n\nMakes sense?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":194,"cache_read_input_tokens":256478,"output_tokens":543,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":194,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":543,"cache_read_input_tokens":256478,"cache_creation_input_tokens":194,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":194},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaNgqF